<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nur Jahan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1577">1577</a>–<a href="/years/1645">1645</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Mehr-un-Nissa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nur Jahan, born Mehr-un-Nissa, was the twentieth wife and chief consort of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>, and is considered by some historians the real power behind the throne for more than a decade. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> in 1577 to the Persian noble Mirza Ghiyas Beg, she married Sher Afgan Khan in 1594 and Jahangir in 1611. She held honours no other Mughal empress enjoyed, including coinage struck in her name, and in 1626 led a unit against the rebel Mahabat Khan, who had captured her husband. After Jahangir died in 1627 the war of succession went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, and she lived under house arrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> until her death in 1645.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>More often women held power through the palace: <b>Nur Jahan</b> held the Mughal imperial seal and had coins struck in her name.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>Mirza Ghiyas Beg reached India from Iran impoverished and held only a modest post until his daughter married Jahangir in 1611 as <b>Nur Jahan</b>; he then became chief minister.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li></ul></section>
